      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To assess the indications, effectiveness, and complications of ovarian transposition before pelvic irradiation for cervical cancer."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Gynecologic oncology department at a French cancer center."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENT(S)",
          "content": "One hundred seven patients treated for cervical cancer."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION(S)",
          "content": "Ovarian transposition to the paracolic gutters with radical hysterectomy and lymphadenectomy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)",
          "content": "Clinical and laboratory follow-up tests for ovarian function."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULT(S)",
          "content": "Bilateral ovarian transposition was achieved in 104 patients (98%). Twelve patients were lost to follow-up or excluded because of evolution of the disease. Preservation of ovarian function was achieved in 83% of the patients having follow-up. The rates of ovarian preservation were 100% for patients treated exclusively by surgery, 90% for patients treated by postoperative vaginal brachytherapy, and 60% for patients treated by postoperative external radiation therapy and vaginal brachytherapy. The main risk for ovarian failure was found in patients treated by external radiation therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION(S)",
          "content": "Ovarian transposition is a safe and effective procedure for preserving ovarian function in patients treated by a radiosurgical combination. This procedure should be performed in patients <40 years of age with a small invasive cervical carcinoma (<3 cm) treated by initial surgery. In such selected cases, the risk of ovarian metastasis is low."
        }
      ]},
